# AwaitIO License

Copyright © 2026 Orukami s.r.o., Rybná 716/24, 110 00 Praha 1, Czech Republic, company ID 64049159, registered in the Commercial Register kept by the Municipal Court in Prague, section C, file 74046 (the "Licensor")

The source code of AwaitIO is public. Whether you can use it for free depends on how many people use it: small teams use AwaitIO for free, including commercially, while for-profit organizations with more users need a Company License. This keeps AwaitIO free for most people and funds its development.

This license covers everything in this repository, the package `awaitio` and every package published by the Licensor under the `@awaitio` scope on npm, now or in the future, such as `@awaitio/input-text`, `@awaitio/input-textarea` and `@awaitio/input-datetime` (together, "AwaitIO").

## Users

A "user" is a person who uses an application built with AwaitIO, whether it runs locally or is deployed: an employee, a contractor or anyone else who opens it, runs its actions or answers them. What counts is the number of real people, not the number of logins: several people sharing one login are several users. Count each person once across all of your organization's AwaitIO applications and environments.

## Organization

Your "organization" is you, if you are an individual, or the legal entity that uses AwaitIO, together with every entity that controls it, is controlled by it or is under common control with it. "Control" means owning more than 50% of the voting shares or other ownership interest, or having the power to direct the entity's management. Users are counted across the whole organization: entities in one group count their users together and cannot each use the Free License separately.

## Free License

### Who is eligible

You may use AwaitIO for free if you are:

- an individual, including a sole trader or a freelancer, or a for-profit organization, in each case with up to 3 users;
- a non-profit or not-for-profit organization, or an educational institution; or
- evaluating whether AwaitIO fits your needs, with any number of users, for 30 days from your organization's first use of AwaitIO. The evaluation period is counted once per organization and does not start again with a new application, environment or version. After it ends, an organization that is not eligible for the Free License otherwise needs a Company License or an Enterprise agreement to keep using AwaitIO.

### What you may do

If you are eligible, you are granted permission, free of charge, to:

- use AwaitIO, commercially or not, including in production;
- build applications and internal tools with AwaitIO, for yourself and for your clients; and
- modify AwaitIO for your own use, or to contribute fixes and improvements back to AwaitIO.

## Company License

An individual or a for-profit organization with more than 3 users needs a Company License to use AwaitIO. It allows the same use as the Free License, in any number of applications and environments. Its price depends on the number of users:

- 4 to 10 users: 50 USD per month per organization;
- 11 to 50 users: 250 USD per month per organization.

An individual or a for-profit organization with more than 50 users needs an Enterprise agreement instead of a Company License (see below).

To get one, sponsor AwaitIO on GitHub Sponsors (https://github.com/sponsors/awaitio) with the monthly Company License tier for your number of users, from your organization's GitHub account or on its behalf. The Company License is valid for as long as the monthly sponsorship is active; when it ends, so does the Company License. When your organization grows past 10 users, move to the higher tier; when it grows past 50 users, agree on an Enterprise agreement.

The Licensor issues an invoice for every payment on request: write to p.homoky@gmail.com with your organization's billing details. For more than 50 users, a written contract, a service-level agreement, priority support or other custom terms, write to the same address to agree on an Enterprise agreement.

Eligibility is decided by the organization that runs the application. If you build tools with AwaitIO for a client and the client runs them, the client's users count and the client needs a Company License unless it is eligible for the Free License itself. People who only develop, test or demonstrate an application for a client, such as the developers of an agency or a freelancer, are not users of it and do not count towards their own organization either. Whoever runs the application for its users pays: if you run it for your client yourself, for example as a hosted back office, its users count as yours. An agency or a freelancer may get the client's Company License on the client's behalf, for example as part of its own price: the license then covers the client, in the tier for the client's number of users, for as long as the sponsorship for it is active.

## Same software, honor system

The Free License and the Company License cover exactly the same software. AwaitIO has no paid features, no license keys, no usage limits and no telemetry, and it never checks which license you have. The licensing works on trust: if your organization needs a Company License, get one.

## Sponsoring

Anyone may sponsor AwaitIO on GitHub Sponsors, once or monthly, with any amount, to support its development. A sponsorship other than a monthly Company License tier is a gift and does not grant a Company License.

## What nobody may do

Whatever your license, without a separate written agreement with the Licensor you may not:

1. copy or modify AwaitIO in order to sell, rent, license, relicense or sublicense AwaitIO itself or your own derivative or fork of it;
2. offer AwaitIO, or a derivative or fork of it, to third parties as a framework, builder or platform for making internal tools, admin panels or back offices, that is, a product whose main purpose is to let others build their own applications;
3. offer to third parties a commercial hosted or managed service built on AwaitIO, such as a dashboard, cloud, monitoring or management service for applications that use AwaitIO.

Building applications with AwaitIO, running them and handing them over to your clients is none of the above. An application with its own purpose, such as the back office of a particular business, or the same application delivered to many clients, is not a framework, builder or platform under point 2, even when it is built with AwaitIO. Neither is making your own components, plugins or extensions for AwaitIO and sharing, selling or licensing them, as long as they do not include AwaitIO itself or a modified copy of it.

